## 1. Biggest gap: you only learn from fail-highs

`updateHistories` runs only when `bestScore >= beta` and `bestMove` is quiet. That throws away two strong signals every modern engine uses:

**(a) Fail-low → bonus for the parent's quiet move.** If this node fails low, the move that led here was a good refutation. Stockfish gives the parent's move a continuation-history bonus (from the parent's perspective). This is the single most valuable ordering update you're missing because fail-low (all-)nodes are the most common node type.

**(b) Quiet malus when a capture is best.** If a bad-noisy or late capture fails high, every quiet tried before it was wrong, but you never penalize them.

Both fit your existing gravity formula. Add a helper that updates the continuation tables *as seen from ply-1*:

```c
// history.c
static void updateContAt(S_BOARD *pos, int backFrom, int piece, int to, int delta){
    // continuation entries for a move made at ply (pos->ply - backFrom)
    for(int slot = 0; slot < CONT_HIST_SLOTS; ++slot){
        int back = backFrom + ContinuationOffsets[slot];
        if(pos->ply < back) continue;
        int pmove = pos->search->moveStack[pos->ply - back];
        if(pmove == NOMOVE || pmove == NULLMOVE) continue;
        int16_t *e = &pos->shared->continuation[slot]
                        [pos->search->pieceStack[pos->ply - back]][TOSQ(pmove)][piece][to];
        *e += HistoryMultiplier * delta - *e * abs(delta) / HistoryDivisor;
    }
}

void bonusPriorQuiet(S_BOARD *pos, int depth){
    if(pos->ply < 1) return;
    int prev = pos->search->moveStack[pos->ply - 1];
    if(prev == NOMOVE || prev == NULLMOVE) return;
    if(pos->st->capturedPiece != EMPTY || (prev & MVFLAGPROM)) return;
    updateContAt(pos, 1, pos->search->pieceStack[pos->ply - 1], TOSQ(prev),
                 MIN(depth * depth, HistoryMax));
}

void malusQuiets(S_BOARD *pos, int *moves, int length, int depth){
    // same body as the quiet loop in updateHistories, delta = -bonus for all
}
```

